<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignleft size-full wp-image-1045601" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2017/12/06210331/endangered-elements-e1512623055752.png" alt width="900" height="636"></p>
<p>The Periodic Table of Elements lists the 118 chemical elements that make up everything in our world. Some you’re familiar with—Hydrogen, Oxygen, Nitrogen, etc. Others maybe less so—Vanadium, Germanium, and Yttrium.</p>
<p>According to <a href="https://www.acs.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the American Chemical Society</a>, 44 of those 118 elements might disappear by century’s end. Enter the <a href="https://www.acs.org/green-chemistry-sustainability/research-innovation/endangered-elements.html">Periodic Table of Endangered Elements</a> (shown above). The most&nbsp;endangered ones, highlighted in red, are Zinc, Gallium,&nbsp;Germanium, Arsenic (is this a good or very bad thing?), Silver, Indium,&nbsp;Tellurium, and&nbsp;Hafnium. Made available under a Creative Commons license, <a href="https://www.acs.org/green-chemistry-sustainability/research-innovation/endangered-elements.html">the Periodic Table of Endangered Elements can be viewed in a larger format here.</a></p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128133" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/30223242/image-2.png" alt width="1024" height="730" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/30223242/image-2.png 1024w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/30223242/image-2-360x257.png 360w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/30223242/image-2-240x171.png 240w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/30223242/image-2-768x548.png 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"></p>
<p>The original image (top of the page), created in 2015, was updated by EuChemS in 2023 (above). The updated table features the current-day availability of all 90 elements, rather than just the endangered 44 elements, adds an axis for sustainability, and flags elements used in smartphone production.</p>
